+\section{Compact homotopies for normal ordering}
+\label{sec:bf-compact-current-homotopies}
+
+The Wick expansion gives a cohomology class of supported observables.
+A chain comparison also needs a compact primitive for its difference
+from the original product.  At three insertions, that primitive itself
+has a quantum correction.  A finite Koszul complex inside the boundary
+observables constructs these primitives in every polynomial degree.
+
+Fix a disk $U=D_R\subset\C$ and finitely many distinct points in $U$.
+Choose disjoint centered disks about these points, with radial cutoffs
+$\chi_a$ equal to one near their centers.  Choose a radial cutoff
+$\chi$ about zero which equals one on a centered closed disk containing
+all the smaller closed disks.
+All cutoffs have compact support in their stated disks.
+Choose a finite list of distinct pairs $(a_i,n_i)$, where $n_i\ge0$
+is a divided holomorphic jet order, and index it by $1\le i\le N$.
+Several pairs may have the same point.  Set
+\[
+ u_i=-\frac{\bar\partial\chi_{a_i}}{(z-a_i)^{n_i+1}},\qquad
+ e_i=-\frac{\bar\partial\chi}{(z-a_i)^{n_i+1}},\qquad
+ f_i=\frac{\chi-\chi_{a_i}}{(z-a_i)^{n_i+1}}.
+\]
+The apparent poles are removable.  All three expressions are smooth
+and compactly supported in $U$, and
+$\bar\partial f_i=u_i-e_i$.
+In the boundary observable complex, $u_i,e_i$ have degree zero,
+while $f_i$ has degree $-1$.
+
+Keep the bulk parameter $\kappa$ and the positive differential
+$D=\bar\partial+\hbar\Delta_{\mu_\kappa}$ fixed.
+Define the symmetric constants
+\[
+ p_{ij}=
+ \begin{cases}
+ \displaystyle\frac{\kappa}{n_i!n_j!}
+ \partial_{a_i}^{n_i}\partial_{a_j}^{n_j}(a_i-a_j)^{-2},
+       &a_i\ne a_j,\\[4pt]
+ 0,&a_i=a_j.
+ \end{cases}
+\]
+In particular, $p_{ii}=0$.  The extracted current has level
+$-\kappa$, so its Wick contraction is $-\hbar p_{ij}$.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Contractions of compact representatives]
+\label{lem:bf-compact-representative-pairings}
+The Cauchy pairing and the quantum cocycle satisfy
+\[
+ \begin{gathered}
+ P_\kappa(u_i,u_j)=p_{ij},\qquad
+ P_\kappa(e_i,u_j)=P_\kappa(e_i,e_j)=0,\\
+ \mu_\kappa(f_i,u_j)=p_{ij},\qquad
+ \mu_\kappa(f_i,e_j)=0.
+ \end{gathered}
+\]
+The pairing $P_\kappa$ is zero on pairs involving a function generator.
+The cocycle $\mu_\kappa$ is zero on two function generators or two
+form generators.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+For distinct centers, the separated Cauchy calculation gives the
+displayed derivative formula for $P_\kappa(u_i,u_j)$.
+For a common center, simultaneous rotation multiplies this pairing
+by weight $-(n_i+n_j+2)$.  The scalar output has weight zero.
+Rotation invariance therefore makes the pairing zero.
+
+The Cauchy transform of an outer representative is
+\[
+ \mathscr C e_i=\frac{1-\chi}{(z-a_i)^{n_i+1}}.
+\]
+Both sides have Dolbeault derivative $e_i$ and tend to zero at
+infinity.  Their difference is an entire function tending to zero,
+so it vanishes.  The displayed expression is zero near the support
+of every $u_j$.  Hence $P_\kappa(e_i,u_j)=0$.
+On the common annulus, each $e_i$ has the convergent expansion
+\[
+ e_i=\sum_{r\ge0}\binom{n_i+r}{r}a_i^r
+            \left(-\frac{\bar\partial\chi}{z^{n_i+r+1}}\right).
+\]
+Each term has strictly positive state weight.  Pairing two such
+terms gives zero by rotation invariance.  Smooth convergence and
+continuity of $P_\kappa$ give $P_\kappa(e_i,e_j)=0$.
+
+The Cauchy identity on compact functions gives
+\[
+ \mu_\kappa(f_i,v)=P_\kappa(\bar\partial f_i,v)
+                 =P_\kappa(u_i-e_i,v)
+\]
+for each form generator $v$.  This proves both cocycle formulas.
+The remaining vanishing statements follow from their specified degrees.
+\end{proof}
+
+Let $x_i,y_i,\theta_i$ denote the observable generators represented
+by $u_i,e_i,f_i$, respectively.  The symbols $f_i$ continue to denote
+the actual compact functions.  Thus
+\[
+ |x_i|=|y_i|=0,\quad |\theta_i|=-1,\qquad
+ Q=\C[x_1,\ldots,x_N,y_1,\ldots,y_N]
+              \otimes\Lambda(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_N)[\hbar].
+\]
+The odd variables anticommute.  Use left derivatives in them, so
+$\partial_{\theta_i}(\theta_{j_1}\cdots\theta_{j_s})$
+has sign $(-1)^{r-1}$ when $i=j_r$ in increasing order.
+All tensor substitutions use these cohomological degrees.
+The map $\rho:Q\to\mathcal B_\kappa(U)$ substitutes the compact
+representatives and multiplies them as observable polynomials.
+
+\begin{proposition}[A quantum complex of compact currents]
+\label{prop:bf-compact-current-complex}
+The map $\rho$ embeds $Q$ as a subcomplex of the full smooth boundary
+observable complex.  Its differential is
+\begin{equation}
+ \label{eq:bf-compact-current-differential}
+ D_Q=\sum_i(x_i-y_i)\partial_{\theta_i}
+   +\hbar\sum_{i<j}p_{ij}
+       (\partial_{x_j}\partial_{\theta_i}
+        +\partial_{x_i}\partial_{\theta_j}).
+\end{equation}
+This assertion concerns a complex of vector spaces over
+$\C[\hbar]$.  The ordinary multiplication on $Q$ is not a
+differential graded multiplication when some $p_{ij}\ne0$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The preceding lemma computes every possible contraction of its
+generators.  Together with $\bar\partial f_i=u_i-e_i$, it gives
+\eqref{eq:bf-compact-current-differential} on every polynomial.
+
+The $u_i$ are linearly independent: their supports separate the
+centers, and the angular Fourier powers separate the orders at one
+center.  Their supports are disjoint from the common outer annulus.
+The $e_i$ are linearly independent there.  Indeed, a relation among
+them would make a rational function
+$\sum_i c_i(z-a_i)^{-n_i-1}$ vanish on an open subset of the annulus.
+It would vanish identically, and its partial fractions give $c_i=0$.
+Thus all the form generators are independent.  A relation among
+the $f_i$ would give a relation among $u_i-e_i$ after differentiation,
+so all function generators are independent as well.
+Symmetric and exterior powers of these finite-dimensional subspaces
+embed into their smooth tensor powers.  For example, continuous dual
+linear functionals selecting their basis vectors supply a left inverse
+on every tensor power, and then on its finite-group coinvariants.
+This proves injectivity of $\rho$ in the stated smooth-kernel convention.
+
+Finally,
+$D_Q(\theta_i x_j)-(D_Q\theta_i)x_j=\hbar p_{ij}$.
+This proves the asserted failure of the Leibniz identity whenever
+the displayed scalar is nonzero.
+\end{proof}
+
+Write $\delta_i=x_i-y_i$, let
+$A=\C[y_1,\ldots,y_N][\hbar]$ with zero differential, and let
+$\iota:A\to Q$ be the inclusion.  Let $\pi:Q\to A$ set all
+$\delta_i,\theta_i$ to zero.  On $Q$ define
+\[
+ d_0=\sum_i\delta_i\partial_{\theta_i},\qquad
+ K_Q=\sum_{i<j}p_{ij}\partial_{x_i}\partial_{x_j},\qquad
+ N_Q=e^{-\hbar K_Q}.
+\]
+The derivatives $\partial_{x_i}$ fix $y_j$ and $\theta_j$.
+For a monomial with total degree $q$ in the variables
+$\delta_i,\theta_i$, define
+\[
+ h_0=\begin{cases}
+ \displaystyle\frac1q\sum_i\theta_i\partial_{\delta_i},&q>0,\\[4pt]
+ 0,&q=0.
+ \end{cases}
+\]
+Extend this definition linearly over $A$.
+All exponentials below terminate on each polynomial.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Compact quantum contraction]
+\label{thm:bf-compact-quantum-contraction}
+The maps
+\[
+ \Pi=\pi N_Q:Q\longrightarrow A,\qquad
+ H=N_Q^{-1}h_0N_Q:Q\longrightarrow Q[-1]
+\]
+and the inclusion $\iota$ satisfy
+\begin{equation}
+ \label{eq:bf-compact-quantum-homotopy}
+ \Pi\iota=1,\qquad D_QH+HD_Q=1-\iota\Pi,
+ \qquad H\iota=\Pi H=H^2=0.
+\end{equation}
+Both $\iota$ and $\Pi$ are chain maps.  Consequently $Q$ has
+cohomology $A$ in degree zero and zero cohomology in every other degree.
+These identities hold for every $\kappa$, including zero.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+The odd derivation $s_0=\sum_i\theta_i\partial_{\delta_i}$ satisfies
+\[
+ d_0s_0+s_0d_0
+   =\sum_i\delta_i\partial_{\delta_i}
+      +\sum_i\theta_i\partial_{\theta_i}.
+\]
+One checks the equality on the generators.  Both sides are even
+derivations, which proves it on every polynomial.  The right side
+acts by $q$ on the specified degree-$q$ summand.  Thus
+$d_0h_0+h_0d_0=1-\iota\pi$.
+The operator $s_0$ preserves $q$ and has square zero, because the
+odd factors anticommute while the even derivatives commute.
+This also gives $h_0^2=h_0\iota=\pi h_0=0$.
+
+Direct commutation gives
+\[
+ [K_Q,d_0]=\sum_{i<j}p_{ij}
+       (\partial_{x_j}\partial_{\theta_i}
+        +\partial_{x_i}\partial_{\theta_j}),\qquad
+ [K_Q,[K_Q,d_0]]=0.
+\]
+Therefore $D_Q=N_Q^{-1}d_0N_Q$.
+Also $N_Q\iota=\iota=N_Q^{-1}\iota$, since $K_Q$ kills
+polynomials in the $y_i$ alone.  Conjugating the classical contraction
+gives every equation in \eqref{eq:bf-compact-quantum-homotopy}.
+It also proves $D_Q^2=0$ and the chain-map assertions.
+No inverse of $\kappa$ or $\hbar$ occurs.
+\end{proof}
+
+The inclusion into the full boundary complex preserves these explicit
+homotopies.  For a polynomial $F(x)$ in the local current generators,
+\begin{equation}
+ \label{eq:bf-compact-wick-chain}
+ F(u)-\iota_U\!\left(
+       \left.e^{-\hbar K_Q}F(x)\right|_{x=y}\right)
+       =D\rho H F(x),
+ \qquad \iota_U=\rho\iota.
+\end{equation}
+Here $F(u)=\rho F(x)$ and $D_QF(x)=0$.
+The equality is in compactly supported observables, before taking
+cohomology.  Distinct input groups have disjoint small disks.
+Their actual factorization product is the ordinary polynomial
+on the left.  Expanding the exponential on the right gives every
+matching between groups, with contraction $-\hbar p_{ij}$.
+Pairings within a group vanish by the common-center convention.
+Repeated factors are counted by the polynomial derivatives.
+
+For example, with $\delta_i=x_i-y_i$ and three different indices,
+\[
+ \begin{aligned}
+ H(x_1x_2)&=\tfrac12\theta_1(x_2+y_2)
+                   +\tfrac12\theta_2(x_1+y_1),\\
+ \Pi(x_1x_2)&=y_1y_2-\hbar p_{12},\\
+ H(x_1x_2x_3)&=
+ \sum_{i=1}^3\theta_i
+  \left(y_jy_k+\frac{\delta_jy_k+y_j\delta_k}{2}
+                   +\frac{\delta_j\delta_k}{3}
+                   -\frac{2\hbar p_{jk}}3\right),\\
+ \Pi(x_1x_2x_3)&=y_1y_2y_3
+       -\hbar(p_{12}y_3+p_{13}y_2+p_{23}y_1).
+ \end{aligned}
+\]
+In the cubic sum, $\{j,k\}=\{1,2,3\}\setminus\{i\}$.
+Its summand is symmetric in $j,k$.
+The classical Euler homotopy contributes the first three terms.
+Applying $N_Q$ before it contributes $-\hbar p_{jk}\theta_i$.
+Applying $N_Q^{-1}$ afterwards contributes
+$\hbar p_{jk}\theta_i/3$.  Their sum gives the printed correction.
+In particular, omitting that correction leaves the defect
+\[
+ \frac{2\hbar}{3}
+       (p_{12}\delta_3+p_{13}\delta_2+p_{23}\delta_1)
+\]
+in the cubic chain identity.  For nonzero covariance, independence of the
+variables $\delta_i$ makes this defect nonzero.
+The binary identity alone cannot fix it.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Split inclusion in smooth boundary observables]
+\label{prop:bf-compact-full-boundary-split}
+There is an explicit chain map
+$L_U:\mathcal B_\kappa(U)\to A$ with $L_U\iota_U=1$.
+Thus the annular polynomial states inject into the cohomology of
+the full smooth boundary observable complex.
+On the subcomplex $\rho Q$, $L_U\rho=\Pi$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Choose holomorphic polynomials $F_i(z)$ such that
+\[
+ \frac{F_i^{(n_j)}(a_j)}{n_j!}=\delta_{ij}
+                   \qquad(1\le i,j\le N).
+\]
+Such polynomials exist by finite interpolation.  To verify existence,
+let $m_a=1+\max\{n_j:a_j=a\}$ and $M=\sum_a m_a$.
+The map from polynomials of degree less than $M$ to their full jets
+of orders less than $m_a$ is injective: a polynomial in its kernel
+is divisible by $\prod_a(z-a)^{m_a}$, of degree $M$.
+The domain and target have dimension $M$, so the map is surjective.
+Prescribe the stated entries and set all other jet entries to zero.
+
+On the classical observable complex, send a form generator $v$ to
+\[
+ R(v)=\sum_i\left(\int_U^{\mathrm{res}}v\wedge F_i(z)dz\right)y_i
+\]
+and a function generator to zero.  Extend by multiplication.
+For a compact smooth kernel, integrate in each variable and take
+the finite sum over its indices $i$.  These integrals are continuous
+on each fixed compact support and respect the symmetric coinvariants.
+Integration by parts and holomorphicity give $Rd=0$.
+The moments of both $u_j$ and $e_j$ give $R(u_j)=R(e_j)=y_j$.
+Thus $R\rho=\pi$ on the classical complexes.
+
+The Cauchy contraction restricts to $K_Q$ on $\rho Q$, by
+Lemma~\ref{lem:bf-compact-representative-pairings}.
+Consequently $N_U\rho=\rho N_Q$.
+Set $L_U=RN_U$, using the chain isomorphism
+of Proposition~\ref{prop:bf-cauchy-conjugation}.
+Then $L_U\rho=\pi N_Q=\Pi$ and $L_U\iota_U=1$.
+\end{proof}
+
+For a fixed set of centers, increasing the finite jet list preserves
+$D_Q,K_Q,h_0,\Pi,H$ on the smaller polynomial algebra.
+Hence the compact contraction also holds on the union of all finite
+jet lists.  Its elements still have finite polynomial degree.
+The interpolation projection $L_U$ requires a finite list and is
+not asserted to be compatible with this union.
+The union has more jet generators, but it does not include arbitrary
+smooth kernels or all infinite moment sequences.
+
+The maps $D_Q,N_Q,H,\Pi$ are polynomial in $\hbar$ on each input
+polynomial.  They therefore extend to $\widehat Q=\varprojlim_m Q/\hbar^mQ$
+and $\widehat A=\varprojlim_m A/\hbar^mA$, where each
+coefficient has finite polynomial degree.  A fixed coefficient of
+an exponential receives finitely many terms.  The same identities
+hold modulo every $\hbar^m$, hence in the inverse limit.
+This is a contraction of the specified completed subcomplex.
+It does not compute the cohomology of the completed full boundary complex.
+
+The compact collar chain map sends
+$\rho H F$ to a primitive for the corresponding bulk--boundary
+product difference.  Its chain identity follows by applying that
+map to \eqref{eq:bf-compact-wick-chain}; its separated-product identity
+identifies the first term with the actual collar product.
+Thus the normalization $-\hbar\kappa(a-b)^{-2}$ and its compact
+primitives pass through the same boundary comparison.
+Construction of operations on collision chains requires further
+maps compatible with those chains and their boundary strata.
